  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest analysis shows front moving through the\nMid-Atlantic and central NC early this afternoon. Later this \nafternoon and evening, dry air advecting in with this reinforcing\nboundary and building high pressure will begin a clearing trend\nfrom NW to SE with TDs diving well into the 50s and even upper \n40s inland by this time Tues AM. Lows will fall into the 50s\ninland and low 60s for the beaches. \n\nHigh pres builds further tomorrow into Wed, resulting in very \npleasant mild and dry days with highs near climo in the 75-80 \nrange (low 70s OBX), but cooler than normal lows well down into \nthe 50s for the mainland, and perhaps a few 40s for the normally\ncooler sheltered locales. The strongest signal for this is on\nWednesday when the high crests overhead and environment is most\nfavored for radiational cooling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mid-level zonal flow returns Thu into the \nweekend, and this will bring a modest warming trend back to \nENC. SW flow returns by Friday as a broad but weak mid-level low\ndevelops and meanders over the southern plains, bringing a \nreturn of Gulf moisture by the end of the week. There are still\nplenty of details to sort out and some timing differences, but \ngeneral ensemble consensus is that frontal boundary still \nstalled to our south will be pulled northward as one or\npotentially multiple lows develop along it and lift northward.\nThis will bring a return for rain chances, and with the moisture\nhaving Gulf origins some heavier rainfall is not implausible.",
